%S A402007 1513,56041,181681,1766521,3376921,4295281,18144361,19628761,70474561,
%T A402007 131638081,220402321,152297161,330521881,211596001,869881681,
%U A402007 911873881,1412473561,1556559481,1320761161,3085919281,1989555961,4492449001,6272610241,7544758321
%N A402007 Half the sum of the fourth powers of the hypotenuse and the sum of the legs: a(n) = (p^4 + (u+v)^4)/2, where u and v are the legs of the primitive Pythagorean triangle with hypotenuse p = A002144(n).
%C A402007 If p = A002144(n) = x^2 + y^2 with integers x > y > 0, then the odd leg is u = x^2 - y^2 and the even leg is v = 2*x*y. Let h = u+v, so a(n) = (p^4+h^4)/2. Either leg may be the longer one. Since p and h are odd, a(n) is an integer.
%C A402007 Terms are listed in increasing order of p, not in increasing order of a(n); for example, a(11) = 220402321 > a(12) = 152297161.
%C A402007 A402006 lists the hypotenuses p for which a(n) is prime. These prime values are half-quartan primes (A002646). Every prime factor of a(n) is congruent to 1 modulo 8 (A007519); see the linked note.
%C A402007 For p = A002144(n), let Q = p*a(n). The construction in A402008 gives a 3 X 3 magic square with central entry Q^2 and nine distinct positive integer entries, at least six of them squares. This works whether a(n) is prime or composite.
%H A402007 Edward Schmidt, <a href="/A402007/b402007.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..2549</a>
%H A402007 Christian Boyer, <a href="http://www.multimagie.com/English/SquaresOfSquares.htm">Magic squares of squares</a>.
%H A402007 Christian Boyer, <a href="https://doi.org/10.1007/BF02985794">Some notes on the magic squares of squares problem</a>, The Mathematical Intelligencer, 27 (2005), 52-64.
%H A402007 Christian Boyer, <a href="http://www.multimagie.com/English/SquaresOfSquaresSearch.htm">Latest research on the "3x3 magic square of squares" problem</a>.
%H A402007 Lee Morgenstern, <a href="http://www.multimagie.com/English/Morgenstern14.htm">Euler's Formulas for 3x3 Magic Squares of 6 Squares</a>, December 2011.
%H A402007 Edward Schmidt, <a href="/A402008/a402008.txt">Half-sums of fourth powers from Pythagorean triangles and a magic square with six squares</a>.
%H A402007 Edward Schmidt, <a href="/A400365/a400365.txt">Fourth powers of Pythagorean legs and a magic square with six squares</a>.
%H A402007 Edward Schmidt, <a href="/A401004/a401004.txt">Pythagorean primes, a Pell-type recurrence and a magic square with six squares</a>.
%H A402007 Edward Schmidt, <a href="/A401446/a401446.txt">A Fibonacci recurrence step on Pythagorean legs and a magic square with six squares</a>.
%H A402007 Edward Schmidt, <a href="/A401742/a401742.txt">Semiperimeter and inradius of Pythagorean triangles and a magic square with six squares</a>.
%F A402007 If p = A002144(n) and u, v are the legs of its primitive Pythagorean triangle, then a(n) = (p^4+(u+v)^4)/2 = p^4 + 2*p^2*u*v + 2*u^2*v^2 = (p^2+u*v)^2 + (u*v)^2.
%F A402007 If p = A002144(n) = x^2 + y^2 with integers x > y > 0, then a(n) = ((x^2+y^2)^4 + (x^2+2*x*y-y^2)^4)/2.
%F A402007 a(n) = (A002144(n)^4 + (A002365(n)+A002366(n))^4)/2.
%F A402007 Here A002365(n) and A002366(n) are the longer and shorter legs, respectively.
%F A402007 a(n) = 2*A002144(n)^2*A401741(n) - A400364(n).
%e A402007 The first Pythagorean prime is 5, whose primitive triangle has legs 3 and 4. Thus a(1) = (5^4+7^4)/2 = 1513 = 17*89.
%e A402007 The next two hypotenuses are 13 and 17, with legs (5, 12) and (8, 15), respectively. Consequently, a(2) = (13^4+17^4)/2 = 56041 and a(3) = (17^4+23^4)/2 = 181681 = 97*1873.
%e A402007 For p = 29, a(4) = (29^4+41^4)/2 = 1766521 = 17*103913.
%o A402007 (Python)
%o A402007 from math import isqrt
%o A402007 from sympy import primerange
%o A402007 def a402007(limit=250):
%o A402007     # Return terms for hypotenuses p < limit.
%o A402007     out = []
%o A402007     for p in primerange(5, limit):
%o A402007         if p % 4 != 1:
%o A402007             continue
%o A402007         for y in range(1, isqrt(p // 2) + 1):
%o A402007             x = isqrt(p - y*y)
%o A402007             if x*x + y*y == p:
%o A402007                 h = x*x - y*y + 2*x*y
%o A402007                 out.append((p**4 + h**4) // 2)
%o A402007                 break
%o A402007     return out
%Y A402007 Cf. A002144, A002365, A002366, A002646, A007519, A400363, A400364, A400365, A401002, A401003, A401004, A401444, A401445, A401446, A401740, A401741, A401742, A402006, A402008.
